.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Sy cmp
command is a client implementation for the Certificate Management
Protocol (CMP) as defined in RFC4210. It can be used to request
certificates from a CA server, update their certificates, request
certificates to be revoked, and perform other types of CMP requests.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -help
Display a summary of all options
.It Sy -config Em filename
Configuration file to use.
An empty string
.Qo Li \(dq" Qc
means none.
Default filename is from the environment variable
.Qo Li OPENSSL_CONF Qc .
.It Sy -section Em names
Section(s) to use within config file defining CMP options.
An empty string
.Qo Li \(dq" Qc
means no specific section.
Default is
.Qo Li cmp Qc .
.Pp
Multiple section names may be given, separated by commas and/or
whitespace (where in the latter case the whole argument must be enclosed
in "..."). Contents of sections named later may override contents of
sections named before.
In any case, as usual, the
.Qo Li [default] Qc
section and finally the unnamed section (as far as present) can provide
per-option fallback values.
.It Sy -verbosity Em level
Level of verbosity for logging, error output, etc.
0 = EMERG, 1 = ALERT, 2 = CRIT, 3 = ERR, 4 = WARN, 5 = NOTE, 6 = INFO, 7
= DEBUG, 8 = TRACE. Defaults to 6 = INFO.
.El
.Ss Generic message options
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -cmd Em ir|cr|kur|p10cr|rr|genm
CMP command to execute.
Currently implemented commands are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It ir - Initialization Request
.It cr - Certificate Request
.It p10cr - PKCS#10 Certification Request (for legacy support)
.It kur - Key Update Request
.It rr - Revocation Request
.It genm - General Message
.El
.Sy ir
requests initialization of an end entity into a PKI hierarchy by issuing
a first certificate.
.Pp
.Sy cr
requests issuing an additional certificate for an end entity already
initialized to the PKI hierarchy.
.Pp
.Sy p10cr
requests issuing an additional certificate similarly to
.Sy cr
but using legacy PKCS#10 CSR format.
.Pp
.Sy kur
requests a (key) update for an existing certificate.
.Pp
.Sy rr
requests revocation of an existing certificate.
.Pp
.Sy genm
requests information using a General Message, where optionally included
.Sy InfoTypeAndValue Ns s
may be used to state which info is of interest.
Upon receipt of the General Response, information about all received
ITAV
.Sy infoType Ns s
is printed to stdout.
.It Sy -infotype Em name
Set InfoType name to use for requesting specific info in
.Sy genm ,
e.g.,
.Qo Li signKeyPairTypes Qc .
There is specific support for
.Qo Li caCerts Qc ,
.Qo Li rootCaCert Qc ,
.Qo Li certReqTemplate Qc ,
and
.Qo Li crlStatusList Qc
(CRL update retrieval).
.It Sy -profile Em name
Name of a certificate profile to place in the PKIHeader generalInfo
field of request messages.
.It Sy -geninfo Em values
A comma-separated list of InfoTypeAndValue to place in the generalInfo
field of the PKIHeader of requests messages.
Each InfoTypeAndValue gives an OID and an integer or string value of the
form
.Em OID Ns :int: Ns Em number
or
.Em OID Ns :str: Ns Em text ,
e.g.,
.Qo Li '1.2.3.4:int:56789, id-kp:str:name' Qc .
.It Sy -template Em filename
The file to save any CRMF certTemplate in DER format received in a genp
message with id-it-certReqTemplate.
.It Sy -keyspec Em filename
It is optional and used to specify the file to save any keySpec if
present in a genp message with id-it-keyGenParameters.
.Pp
Note: any keySpec field contents received are logged as INFO.
.El
.Ss Certificate enrollment options
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -newkey Em filename Ns | Ns Em uri
The source of the private or public key for the certificate being
requested.
Defaults to the public key in the PKCS#10 CSR given with the
.Sy -csr
option, the public key of the reference certificate, or the current
client key.
.Pp
The public portion of the key is placed in the certification request.
.Pp
Unless
.Sy -cmd
.Em p10cr ,
.Sy -popo
.Em -1 ,
or
.Sy -popo
.Em 0
is given, the private key will be needed as well to provide the proof of
possession (POPO), where the
.Sy -key
option may provide a fallback.
.It Sy -newkeypass Em arg
Pass phrase source for the key given with the
.Sy -newkey
option.
If not given here, the password will be prompted for if needed.
.Pp
For more information about the format of
.Em arg
see
.Xr openssl-passphrase-options 1 .
.It Sy -centralkeygen
Request central key generation for certificate enrollment.
This applies to
.Sy -cmd
.Em ir|cr|kur|p10cr .
.It Sy -newkeyout Em filename
File to save centrally generated private key, in PEM format.
.It Sy -subject Em name
X.509 Distinguished Name (DN) to use as subject field in the requested
certificate template in IR/CR/KUR messages.
If the NULL-DN
.Pf ( Qo Li / Qc )
is given then no subject is placed in the template.
Default is the subject DN of any PKCS#10 CSR given with the
.Sy -csr
option.
For KUR, a further fallback is the subject DN of the reference
certificate (see
.Sy -oldcert )
if provided.
This fallback is used for IR and CR only if no SANs are set.
.Pp
If provided and neither of
.Sy -cert ,
.Sy -oldcert ,
or
.Sy -csr
is given, the subject DN is used as fallback sender of outgoing CMP
messages.
.Pp
The argument must be formatted as
.Em /type0=value0/type1=value1/type2=... .
Special characters may be escaped by
.Qo Li \e Qc
(backslash); whitespace is retained.
Empty values are permitted, but the corresponding type will not be
included.
Giving a single
.Qo Li / Qc
will lead to an empty sequence of RDNs (a NULL-DN). Multi-valued RDNs
can be formed by placing a
.Qo Li + Qc
character instead of a
.Qo Li / Qc
between the AttributeValueAssertions (AVAs) that specify the members of
the set.
Example:
.Pp
.Qo Li /DC=org/DC=OpenSSL/DC=users/UID=123456+CN=John Doe Qc
.It Sy -days Em number
Number of days the new certificate is requested to be valid for,
counting from the current time of the host.
Also triggers the explicit request that the validity period starts from
the current time (as seen by the host).
.It Sy -reqexts Em name
Name of section in OpenSSL config file defining certificate request
extensions.
If the
.Sy -csr
option is present, these extensions augment the extensions contained the
given PKCS#10 CSR, overriding any extensions with same OIDs.
.It Sy -sans Em spec
One or more IP addresses, email addresses, DNS names, or URIs separated
by commas or whitespace (where in the latter case the whole argument
must be enclosed in "...") to add as Subject Alternative Name(s) (SAN)
certificate request extension.
If the special element "critical" is given the SANs are flagged as
critical.
Cannot be used if any Subject Alternative Name extension is set via
.Sy -reqexts .
.It Sy -san_nodefault
When Subject Alternative Names are not given via
.Sy -sans
nor defined via
.Sy -reqexts ,
they are copied by default from the reference certificate (see
.Sy -oldcert Ns ).
This can be disabled by giving the
.Sy -san_nodefault
option.
.It Sy -policies Em name
Name of section in OpenSSL config file defining policies to be set as
certificate request extension.
This option cannot be used together with
.Sy -policy_oids .
.It Sy -policy_oids Em names
One or more OID(s), separated by commas and/or whitespace (where in the
latter case the whole argument must be enclosed in "...") to add as
certificate policies request extension.
This option cannot be used together with
.Sy -policies .
.It Sy -policy_oids_critical
Flag the policies given with
.Sy -policy_oids
as critical.
.It Sy -popo Em number
Proof-of-possession (POPO) method to use for IR/CR/KUR; values:
.Qo Li -1 Qc Ns ..<2>
where
.Qo Li -1 Qc
= NONE, which implies central key generation,
.Qo Li 0 Qc
= RAVERIFIED,
.Qo Li 1 Qc
= SIGNATURE (default),
.Qo Li 2 Qc
= KEYENC.
.Pp
Note that a signature-based POPO can only be produced if a private key
is provided via the
.Sy -newkey
or
.Sy -key
options.
.It Sy -csr Em filename
PKCS#10 CSR in PEM or DER format containing a certificate request.
With
.Sy -cmd
.Em p10cr
it is used directly in a legacy P10CR message.
.Pp
When used with
.Sy -cmd
.Em ir ,
.Em cr ,
or
.Em kur ,
it is transformed into the respective regular CMP request.
In this case, a private key must be provided (with
.Sy -newkey
or
.Sy -key )
for the proof of possession (unless
.Sy -popo
.Em -1
or
.Sy -popo
.Em 0
is used) and the respective public key is placed in the certification
request (rather than taking over the public key contained in the PKCS#10
CSR).
.Pp
PKCS#10 CSR input may also be used with
.Sy -cmd
.Em rr
to specify the certificate to be revoked via the included subject name
and public key.
Its subject is used as fallback sender in CMP message headers if
.Sy -cert
and
.Sy -oldcert
are not given.
.It Sy -out_trusted Em filenames Ns | Ns Em uris
Trusted certificate(s) to use for validating the newly enrolled
certificate.
During this verification, any certificate status checking is disabled.
.Pp
Multiple sources may be given, separated by commas and/or whitespace
(where in the latter case the whole argument must be enclosed in "...").
Each source may contain multiple certificates.
.Pp
The certificate verification options
.Sy -verify_hostname ,
.Sy -verify_ip ,
and
.Sy -verify_email
only affect the certificate verification enabled via this option.
.It Sy -implicit_confirm
Request implicit confirmation of newly enrolled certificates.
.It Sy -disable_confirm
Do not send certificate confirmation message for newly enrolled
certificate without requesting implicit confirmation to cope with broken
servers not supporting implicit confirmation correctly.
.Sy WARNING:
This leads to behavior violating RFC 4210.
.It Sy -certout Em filename
The file where any newly enrolled certificate should be saved.
.It Sy -chainout Em filename
The file where the chain of any newly enrolled certificate should be
saved.
This chain excludes the leaf certificate, i.e., the newly enrolled
certificate.
Also the trust anchor (the root certificate) is not included.
.Pp
If the
.Sy -certout
option is given, too, with equal
.Em filename
argument, then the file produced contains both outputs concatenated: the
newly enrolled certificate followed by its chain.
.El
.Ss Certificate enrollment and revocation options
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -oldcert Em filename Ns | Ns Em uri
The certificate to be updated (i.e., renewed or re-keyed) in Key Update
Request (KUR) messages or to be revoked in Revocation Request (RR)
messages.
For KUR the certificate to be updated defaults to
.Sy -cert ,
and the resulting certificate is called
.Em reference certificate .
For RR the certificate to be revoked can also be specified using
.Sy -csr .
.Sy -oldcert
and
.Sy -csr
is ignored if
.Sy -issuer
and
.Sy -serial
is provided.
.Pp
The reference certificate, if any, is also used for deriving default
subject DN and Subject Alternative Names and the default issuer entry in
the requested certificate template of an IR/CR/KUR. Its public key is
used as a fallback in the template of certification requests.
Its subject is used as sender of outgoing messages if
.Sy -cert
is not given.
Its issuer is used as default recipient in CMP message headers if
neither
.Sy -recipient ,
.Sy -srvcert ,
nor
.Sy -issuer
is given.
.It Sy -issuer Em name
X.509 Distinguished Name (DN) to place as the issuer field in the
requested certificate template in IR/CR/KUR/RR messages.
If the NULL-DN
.Pf ( Qo Li / Qc )
is given then no issuer is placed in the template.
.Pp
If provided and neither
.Sy -recipient
nor
.Sy -srvcert
is given, the issuer DN is used as fallback recipient of outgoing CMP
messages.
.Pp
The argument must be formatted as
.Em /type0=value0/type1=value1/type2=... .
For details see the description of the
.Sy -subject
option.
.It Sy -serial Em number
Specify the Serial number of certificate to be revoked in revocation
request.
The serial number can be decimal or hex (if preceded by
.Qo Li 0x Qc )
.It Sy -revreason Em number
Set CRLReason to be included in revocation request (RR); values:
.Qo Li 0 Qc Ns .. Ns Qo Li 10 Qc
or
.Qo Li -1 Qc
for none (which is the default).
.Pp
Reason numbers defined in RFC 5280 are:
.Bd -literal
   CRLReason ::= ENUMERATED {
        unspecified             (0),
        keyCompromise           (1),
        cACompromise            (2),
        affiliationChanged      (3),
        superseded              (4),
        cessationOfOperation    (5),
        certificateHold         (6),
        -- value 7 is not used
        removeFromCRL           (8),
        privilegeWithdrawn      (9),
        aACompromise           (10)
    }
.Ed
.El
.Ss Message transfer options
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -server Em [http[s]://][userinfo@]host[:port][/path][?query][#fragment]
The
.Em host
domain name or IP address and optionally
.Em port
of the CMP server to connect to using HTTP(S). IP address may be for v4
or v6, such as
.Qo Li 127.0.0.1 Qc
or
.Qo Li [::1] Qc
for localhost.
If the host string is an IPv6 address, it must be enclosed in
.Qo Li [ Qc
and
.Qo Li ] Qc .
.Pp
This option excludes
.Em -port
and
.Em -use_mock_srv .
It is ignored if
.Em -rspin
is given with enough filename arguments.
.Pp
If the scheme
.Qo Li https Qc
is given, the
.Sy -tls_used
option is implied.
When TLS is used, the default port is 443, otherwise 80. The optional
userinfo and fragment components are ignored.
Any given query component is handled as part of the path component.
If a path is included it provides the default value for the
.Sy -path
option.
.It Sy -proxy Em [http[s]://][userinfo@]host[:port][/path][?query][#fragment]
The HTTP(S) proxy server to use for reaching the CMP server unless
.Sy -no_proxy
applies, see below.
If the host string is an IPv6 address, it must be enclosed in
.Qo Li [ Qc
and
.Qo Li ] Qc .
The proxy port defaults to 80 or 443 if the scheme is
.Qo Li https Qc ;
apart from that the optional
.Qo Li http:// Qc
or
.Qo Li https:// Qc
prefix is ignored (note that using TLS may be required by
.Sy -tls_used
or
.Sy -server
with the prefix
.Qo Li https Qc Ns ),
as well as any path, userinfo, and query, and fragment components.
Defaults to the environment variable
.Qo Li http_proxy Qc
if set, else
.Qo Li HTTP_PROXY Qc
in case no TLS is used, otherwise
.Qo Li https_proxy Qc
if set, else
.Qo Li HTTPS_PROXY Qc .
This option is ignored if
.Em -server
is not given.
.It Sy -no_proxy Em addresses
List of IP addresses and/or DNS names of servers not to use an HTTP(S)
proxy for, separated by commas and/or whitespace (where in the latter
case the whole argument must be enclosed in "..."). Default is from the
environment variable
.Qo Li no_proxy Qc
if set, else
.Qo Li NO_PROXY Qc .
This option is ignored if
.Em -server
is not given.
.It Sy -recipient Em name
Distinguished Name (DN) to use in the recipient field of CMP request
message headers, i.e., the CMP server (usually the addressed CA).
.Pp
The recipient field in the header of a CMP message is mandatory.
If not given explicitly the recipient is determined in the following
order: the subject of the CMP server certificate given with the
.Sy -srvcert
option, the
.Sy -issuer
option, the issuer of the certificate given with the
.Sy -oldcert
option, the issuer of the CMP client certificate
.Pf ( Sy -cert
option), as far as any of those is present, else the NULL-DN as last
resort.
.Pp
The argument must be formatted as
.Em /type0=value0/type1=value1/type2=... .
For details see the description of the
.Sy -subject
option.
.It Sy -path Em remote_path
HTTP path at the CMP server (aka CMP alias) to use for POST requests.
Defaults to any path given with
.Sy -server ,
else
.Qo Li \(dq/" Qc .
.It Sy -keep_alive Em value
If the given value is 0 then HTTP connections are closed after each
response (which would be the default behavior of HTTP 1.0) even if a CMP
transaction needs more than one round trip.
If the value is 1 or 2 then for each transaction a persistent connection
is requested.
If the value is 2 then a persistent connection is required, i.e., an
error occurs if the server does not grant it.
The default value is 1, which means preferring to keep the connection
open.
.It Sy -msg_timeout Em seconds
Number of seconds a CMP request-response message round trip is allowed
to take before a timeout error is returned.
A value <= 0 means no limitation (waiting indefinitely).
Default is to use the
.Sy -total_timeout
setting.
.It Sy -total_timeout Em seconds
Maximum total number of seconds a transaction may take, including
polling etc.
A value <= 0 means no limitation (waiting indefinitely).
Default is 0.
.El
.Ss Server authentication options
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -trusted Em filenames Ns | Ns Em uris
The certificate(s), typically of root CAs, the client shall use as trust
anchors when validating signature-based protection of CMP response
messages.
This option is ignored if the
.Sy -srvcert
option is given as well.
It provides more flexibility than
.Sy -srvcert
because the CMP protection certificate of the server is not pinned but
may be any certificate from which a chain to one of the given trust
anchors can be constructed.
.Pp
If none of
.Sy -trusted ,
.Sy -srvcert ,
and
.Sy -secret
is given, message validation errors will be thrown unless
.Sy -unprotected_errors
permits an exception.
.Pp
Multiple sources may be given, separated by commas and/or whitespace
(where in the latter case the whole argument must be enclosed in "...").
Each source may contain multiple certificates.
.Pp
The certificate verification options
.Sy -verify_hostname ,
.Sy -verify_ip ,
and
.Sy -verify_email
have no effect on the certificate verification enabled via this option.
.It Sy -untrusted Em filenames Ns | Ns Em uris
Non-trusted intermediate CA certificate(s).
Any extra certificates given with the
.Sy -cert
option are appended to it.
All these certificates may be useful for cert path construction for the
own CMP signer certificate (to include in the extraCerts field of
request messages) and for the TLS client certificate (if TLS is used) as
well as for chain building when validating server certificates (checking
signature-based CMP message protection) and when validating newly
enrolled certificates.
.Pp
Multiple sources may be given, separated by commas and/or whitespace
(where in the latter case the whole argument must be enclosed in "...").
Each source may contain multiple certificates.
.It Sy -srvcert Em filename Ns | Ns Em uri
The specific CMP server certificate to expect and directly trust (even
if it is expired) when verifying signature-based protection of CMP
response messages.
This pins the accepted server and results in ignoring the
.Sy -trusted
option.
.Pp
If set, the subject of the certificate is also used as default value for
the recipient of CMP requests and as default value for the expected
sender of CMP responses.
.It Sy -expect_sender Em name
Distinguished Name (DN) expected in the sender field of incoming CMP
messages.
Defaults to the subject DN of the pinned
.Sy -srvcert ,
if any.
.Pp
This can be used to make sure that only a particular entity is accepted
as CMP message signer, and attackers are not able to use arbitrary
certificates of a trusted PKI hierarchy to fraudulently pose as a CMP
server.
Note that this option gives slightly more freedom than setting the
.Sy -srvcert ,
which pins the server to the holder of a particular certificate, while
the expected sender name will continue to match after updates of the
server cert.
.Pp
The argument must be formatted as
.Em /type0=value0/type1=value1/type2=... .
For details see the description of the
.Sy -subject
option.
.It Sy -ignore_keyusage
Ignore key usage restrictions in CMP signer certificates when validating
signature-based protection of incoming CMP messages.
By default,
.Qo Li digitalSignature Qc
must be allowed by CMP signer certificates.
This option applies to both CMP clients and the mock server.
.It Sy -unprotected_errors
Accept missing or invalid protection of negative responses from the
server.
This applies to the following message types and contents:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It * error messages
.It * negative certificate responses (IP/CP/KUP)
.It * negative revocation responses (RP)
.It * negative PKIConf messages
.El
.Sy WARNING:
This setting leads to unspecified behavior and it is meant exclusively
to allow interoperability with server implementations violating RFC
4210, e.g.:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It * section 5.1.3.1 allows exceptions from protecting only for special cases: \(dqThere MAY be cases in which the PKIProtection BIT STRING is deliberately not used to protect a message [...] because other protection, external to PKIX, will be applied instead."
.It * section 5.3.21 is clear on ErrMsgContent: \(dqThe CA MUST always sign it with a signature key."
.It * appendix D.4 shows PKIConf message having protection
.El
.It Sy -no_cache_extracerts
Do not cache certificates in the extraCerts field of CMP messages
received.
By default, they are kept as they may be helful for validating further
messages.
This option applies to both CMP clients and the mock server.
.It Sy -srvcertout Em filename
The file where to save the successfully validated certificate, if any,
that the CMP server used for signature-based response message
protection.
If there is no such certificate, typically because the protection was
MAC-based, this is indicated by deleting the file (if it existed).
.It Sy -extracertsout Em filename
The file where to save the list of certificates contained in the
extraCerts field of the last received response message that is not a
pollRep nor PKIConf.
.It Sy -cacertsout Em filename
The file where to save the list of CA certificates contained in the
caPubs field if a positive certificate response (i.e., IP, CP, or KUP)
message was received or contained in a general response (genp) message
with infoType
.Qo Li caCerts Qc .
.It Sy -oldwithold Em filename
The root CA certificate to include in a genm request of infoType
.Qo Li rootCaCert Qc .
If present and the optional oldWithNew certificate is received, it is
verified using the newWithNew certificate as the (only) trust anchor.
.It Sy -newwithnew Em filename
This option must be provided when
.Sy -infotype
.Em rootCaCert
is given.
It specifies the file to save the newWithNew certificate received in a
genp message of type
.Qo Li rootCaKeyUpdate Qc .
If on success no such cert was received, this file (if present) is
deleted to indicate that the requested root CA certificate update is not
available.
.Pp
Any received newWithNew certificate is verified using any received
newWithOld certificate as untrusted intermediate certificate and the
certificate provided with
.Sy -oldwithold
as the (only) trust anchor, or if not provided, using the certificates
given with the
.Sy -trusted
option.
.Pp
.Sy WARNING:
The newWithNew certificate is meant to be a certificate that will be
trusted.
The trust placed in it cannot be stronger than the trust placed in the
.Sy -oldwithold
certificate if present, otherwise it cannot be stronger than the weakest
trust placed in any of the
.Sy -trusted
certificates.
.It Sy -newwithold Em filename
The file to save any newWithOld certificate received in a genp message
of infoType
.Qo Li rootCaKeyUpdate Qc .
If on success no such cert was received, this is indicated by deleting
the file.
.It Sy -oldwithnew Em filename
The file to save any oldWithNew certificate received in a genp message
of infoType
.Qo Li rootCaKeyUpdate Qc .
If on success no such cert was received, this is indicated by deleting
the file.
.It Sy -crlcert Em filename
Certificate to derive CRL issuer data for the source field when
obtaining a CRL in a genm request with infoType
.Qo Li crlStatusList Qc .
Any available distribution point name is preferred over issuer names.
.It Sy -oldcrl Em filename
The CRL to obtain an update for in a genm request with infoType
.Qo Li crlStatusList Qc .
Unless the
.Sy -crlcert
option is provided as well, the given CRL is used for deriving CRL
issuer data for the source field.
Any available distribution point name is preferred over issuer names.
If the CRL contains a thisUpdate field, its value is copied to the
request.
.It Sy -crlout Em filename
The file to save any CRL received in a genp message of infoType
.Qo Li crls Qc .
If on success no such CRL was received, this is indicated by deleting
the file.
.El
.Ss Client authentication options
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -ref Em value
Reference number/string/value to use as fallback senderKID; this is
required if no sender name can be determined from the
.Sy -cert
or <-subject> options and is typically used when authenticating with
pre-shared key (password-based MAC).
.It Sy -secret Em arg
Provides the source of a secret value to use with MAC-based message
protection.
This takes precedence over the
.Sy -cert
and
.Sy -key
options.
The secret is used for creating MAC-based protection of outgoing
messages and for validating incoming messages that have MAC-based
protection.
The algorithm used by default is Password-Based Message Authentication
Code (PBM) as defined in RFC 4210 section 5.1.3.1.
.Pp
For more information about the format of
.Em arg
see
.Xr openssl-passphrase-options 1 .
.It Sy -cert Em filename Ns | Ns Em uri
The client's current CMP signer certificate.
Requires the corresponding key to be given with
.Sy -key .
.Pp
The subject and the public key contained in this certificate serve as
fallback values in the certificate template of IR/CR/KUR messages.
.Pp
The subject of this certificate will be used as sender of outgoing CMP
messages, while the subject of
.Sy -oldcert
or
.Sy -subjectName
may provide fallback values.
.Pp
The issuer of this certificate is used as one of the recipient fallback
values and as fallback issuer entry in the certificate template of
IR/CR/KUR messages.
.Pp
When performing signature-based message protection, this "protection
certificate", also called "signer certificate", will be included first
in the extraCerts field of outgoing messages and the signature is done
with the corresponding key.
In Initialization Request (IR) messages this can be used for
authenticating using an external entity certificate as defined in
appendix E.7 of RFC 4210.
.Pp
For Key Update Request (KUR) messages this is also used as the
certificate to be updated if the
.Sy -oldcert
option is not given.
.Pp
If the file includes further certs, they are appended to the untrusted
certs because they typically constitute the chain of the client
certificate, which is included in the extraCerts field in
signature-protected request messages.
.It Sy -own_trusted Em filenames Ns | Ns Em uris
If this list of certificates is provided then the chain built for the
client-side CMP signer certificate given with the
.Sy -cert
option is verified using the given certificates as trust anchors.
.Pp
Multiple sources may be given, separated by commas and/or whitespace
(where in the latter case the whole argument must be enclosed in "...").
Each source may contain multiple certificates.
.Pp
The certificate verification options
.Sy -verify_hostname ,
.Sy -verify_ip ,
and
.Sy -verify_email
have no effect on the certificate verification enabled via this option.
.It Sy -key Em filename Ns | Ns Em uri
The corresponding private key file for the client's current certificate
given in the
.Sy -cert
option.
This will be used for signature-based message protection unless the
.Sy -secret
option indicating MAC-based protection or
.Sy -unprotected_requests
is given.
.Pp
It is also used as a fallback for the
.Sy -newkey
option with IR/CR/KUR messages.
.It Sy -keypass Em arg
Pass phrase source for the private key given with the
.Sy -key
option.
Also used for
.Sy -cert
and
.Sy -oldcert
in case it is an encrypted PKCS#12 file.
If not given here, the password will be prompted for if needed.
.Pp
For more information about the format of
.Em arg
see
.Xr openssl-passphrase-options 1 .
.It Sy -digest Em name
Specifies name of supported digest to use in RFC 4210's MSG_SIG_ALG and
as the one-way function (OWF) in
.Qo Li MSG_MAC_ALG Qc .
If applicable, this is used for message protection and
proof-of-possession (POPO) signatures.
To see the list of supported digests, use
.Qo Li openssl list -digest-commands Qc .
Defaults to
.Qo Li sha256 Qc .
.It Sy -mac Em name
Specifies the name of the MAC algorithm in
.Qo Li MSG_MAC_ALG Qc .
To get the names of supported MAC algorithms use
.Qo Li openssl list -mac-algorithms Qc
and possibly combine such a name with the name of a supported digest
algorithm, e.g., hmacWithSHA256. Defaults to
.Qo Li hmac-sha1 Qc
as per RFC 4210.
.It Sy -extracerts Em filenames Ns | Ns Em uris
Certificates to append in the extraCerts field when sending messages.
They can be used as the default CMP signer certificate chain to include.
.Pp
Multiple sources may be given, separated by commas and/or whitespace
(where in the latter case the whole argument must be enclosed in "...").
Each source may contain multiple certificates.
.It Sy -unprotected_requests
Send request messages without CMP-level protection.
.El
.Ss Credentials format options
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -certform Em PEM|DER
File format to use when saving a certificate to a file.
Default value is PEM.
.It Sy -crlform Em PEM|DER
File format to use when saving a CRL to a file.
Default value is DER. DER format is preferred because it enables more
efficient storage of large CRLs.
.It Sy -keyform Em PEM|DER|P12|ENGINE
The format of the key input; unspecified by default.
See
.Xr openssl 1
for details.
.It Sy -otherpass Em arg
Pass phrase source for certificate given with the
.Sy -trusted ,
.Sy -untrusted ,
.Sy -own_trusted ,
.Sy -srvcert ,
.Sy -crlcert ,
.Sy -out_trusted ,
.Sy -extracerts ,
.Sy -srv_trusted ,
.Sy -srv_untrusted ,
.Sy -ref_cert ,
.Sy -rsp_extracerts ,
.Sy -rsp_capubs ,
.Sy -rsp_newwithnew ,
.Sy -rsp_newwithold ,
.Sy -rsp_oldwithnew ,
.Sy -tls_extra ,
and
.Sy -tls_trusted
options.
If not given here, the password will be prompted for if needed.
.Pp
For more information about the format of
.Em arg
see
.Xr openssl-passphrase-options 1 .
.It Sy -engine Em id
See
.Xr openssl 1 .
This option is deprecated.
.Pp
As an alternative to using this combination:
.Bd -literal
    -engine {engineid} -key {keyid} -keyform ENGINE
.Ed
.Pp
\&... it's also possible to just give the key ID in URI form to
.Sy -key ,
like this:
.Bd -literal
    -key org.openssl.engine:{engineid}:{keyid}
.Ed
.Pp
This applies to all options specifying keys:
.Sy -key ,
.Sy -newkey ,
and
.Sy -tls_key .
.El
.Ss Provider options
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -provider Em name
.It Sy -provider-path Em path
.It Sy -provparam Em [name:]key=value
.It Sy -propquery Em propq
See
.Xr openssl 1 ,
.Xr provider 7 ,
and
.Xr property 7 .
.El
.Ss Random state options
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -rand Em files Ns , Sy -writerand Em file
See
.Xr openssl 1
for details.
.El
.Ss TLS connection options
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -tls_used
Make the CMP client use TLS (regardless if other TLS-related options are
set) for message exchange with the server via HTTP. This option is not
supported with the
.Em -port
option.
It is implied if the
.Sy -server
option is given with the scheme
.Qo Li https Qc .
It is ignored if the
.Sy -server
option is not given or
.Sy -use_mock_srv
is given or
.Sy -rspin
is given with enough filename arguments.
.Pp
The following TLS-related options are ignored if TLS is not used.
.It Sy -tls_cert Em filename Ns | Ns Em uri
Client's TLS certificate to use for authenticating to the TLS server.
If the source includes further certs they are used (along with
.Sy -untrusted
certs) for constructing the client cert chain provided to the TLS
server.
.It Sy -tls_key Em filename Ns | Ns Em uri
Private key for the client's TLS certificate.
.It Sy -tls_keypass Em arg
Pass phrase source for client's private TLS key
.Sy -tls_key .
Also used for
.Sy -tls_cert
in case it is an encrypted PKCS#12 file.
If not given here, the password will be prompted for if needed.
.Pp
For more information about the format of
.Em arg
see
.Xr openssl-passphrase-options 1 .
.It Sy -tls_extra Em filenames Ns | Ns Em uris
Extra certificates to provide to the TLS server during handshake.
.It Sy -tls_trusted Em filenames Ns | Ns Em uris
Trusted certificate(s) to use for validating the TLS server certificate.
This implies hostname validation.
.Pp
Multiple sources may be given, separated by commas and/or whitespace
(where in the latter case the whole argument must be enclosed in "...").
Each source may contain multiple certificates.
.Pp
The certificate verification options
.Sy -verify_hostname ,
.Sy -verify_ip ,
and
.Sy -verify_email
have no effect on the certificate verification enabled via this option.
.It Sy -tls_host Em name
Address to be checked during hostname validation.
This may be a DNS name or an IP address.
If not given it defaults to the
.Sy -server
address.
.El
.Ss Client-side options for debugging and offline scenarios
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -batch
Do not interactively prompt for input, for instance when a password is
needed.
This can be useful for batch processing and testing.
.It Sy -repeat Em number
Invoke the command the given positive number of times with the same
parameters.
Default is one invocation.
.It Sy -reqin Em filenames
Take the sequence of CMP requests to send to the server from the given
file(s) rather than from the sequence of requests produced internally.
.Pp
This option is useful for supporting offline scenarios where the
certificate request (or any other CMP request) is produced beforehand
and sent out later.
.Pp
This option is ignored if the
.Sy -rspin
option is given because in the latter case no requests are actually
sent.
.Pp
Note that in any case the client produces internally its sequence of CMP
request messages.
Thus, all options required for doing this (such as
.Sy -cmd
and all options providing the required parameters) need to be given also
when the
.Sy -reqin
option is present.
.Pp
If the
.Sy -reqin
option is given for a certificate request and no
.Sy -newkey ,
.Sy -key ,
.Sy -oldcert ,
or
.Sy -csr
option is given, a fallback public key is taken from the request message
file (if it is included in the certificate template).
.Pp
Hint: In case the
.Sy -reqin
option is given for a certificate request, there are situations where
the client has access to the public key to be certified but not to the
private key that by default will be needed for proof of possession.
In this case the POPO is not actually needed (because the internally
produced certificate request message will not be sent), and its
generation can be disabled using the options
.Sy -popo
.Em -1
or
.Sy -popo
.Em 0 .
.Pp
Multiple filenames may be given, separated by commas and/or whitespace
(where in the latter case the whole argument must be enclosed in "...").
.Pp
The files are read as far as needed to complete the transaction and
filenames have been provided.
If more requests are needed, the remaining ones are taken from the items
at the respective position in the sequence of requests produced
internally.
.Pp
The client needs to update the recipNonce field in the given requests
(except for the first one) in order to satisfy the checks to be
performed by the server.
This causes re-protection (if protecting requests is required).
.It Sy -reqin_new_tid
Use a fresh transactionID for CMP request messages read using
.Sy -reqin ,
which causes their reprotection (if protecting requests is required).
This may be needed in case the sequence of requests is reused and the
CMP server complains that the transaction ID has already been used.
.It Sy -reqout Em filenames
Save the sequence of CMP requests created by the client to the given
file(s).
These requests are not sent to the server if the
.Sy -reqin
option is used, too.
.Pp
Multiple filenames may be given, separated by commas and/or whitespace.
.Pp
Files are written as far as needed to save the transaction and filenames
have been provided.
If the transaction contains more requests, the remaining ones are not
saved.
.It Sy -reqout_only Em filename
Save the first CMP requests created by the client to the given file and
exit.
Any options related to CMP servers and their responses are ignored.
.Pp
This option is useful for supporting offline scenarios where the
certificate request (or any other CMP request) is produced beforehand
and sent out later.
.It Sy -rspin Em filenames
Process the sequence of CMP responses provided in the given file(s), not
contacting any given server, as long as enough filenames are provided to
complete the transaction.
.Pp
Multiple filenames may be given, separated by commas and/or whitespace.
.Pp
Any server specified via the
.Em -server
or
.Em -use_mock_srv
options is contacted only if more responses are needed to complete the
transaction.
In this case the transaction will fail unless the server has been
prepared to continue the already started transaction.
.It Sy -rspout Em filenames
Save the sequence of actually used CMP responses to the given file(s).
These have been received from the server unless
.Sy -rspin
takes effect.
.Pp
Multiple filenames may be given, separated by commas and/or whitespace.
.Pp
Files are written as far as needed to save the responses contained in
the transaction and filenames have been provided.
If the transaction contains more responses, the remaining ones are not
saved.
.It Sy -use_mock_srv
Test the client using the internal CMP server mock-up at API level,
bypassing socket-based transfer via HTTP. This excludes the
.Sy -server
and
.Sy -port
options.
.El
.Ss Mock server options
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -port Em number
Act as HTTP-based CMP server mock-up listening on the given local port.
The client may address the server via, e.g.,
.Qo Li 127.0.0.1 Qc
or
.Qo Li [::1] Qc .
This option excludes the
.Sy -server
and
.Sy -use_mock_srv
options.
The
.Sy -rspin ,
.Sy -rspout ,
.Sy -reqin ,
and
.Sy -reqout
options so far are not supported in this mode.
.It Sy -max_msgs Em number
Maximum number of CMP (request) messages the CMP HTTP server mock-up
should handle, which must be nonnegative.
The default value is 0, which means that no limit is imposed.
In any case the server terminates on internal errors, but not when it
detects a CMP-level error that it can successfully answer with an error
message.
.It Sy -srv_ref Em value
Reference value to use as senderKID of server in case no
.Sy -srv_cert
is given.
.It Sy -srv_secret Em arg
Password source for server authentication with a pre-shared key
(secret).
.It Sy -srv_cert Em filename Ns | Ns Em uri
Certificate of the server.
.It Sy -srv_key Em filename Ns | Ns Em uri
Private key used by the server for signing messages.
.It Sy -srv_keypass Em arg
Server private key (and cert) file pass phrase source.
.It Sy -srv_trusted Em filenames Ns | Ns Em uris
Trusted certificates for client authentication.
.Pp
The certificate verification options
.Sy -verify_hostname ,
.Sy -verify_ip ,
and
.Sy -verify_email
have no effect on the certificate verification enabled via this option.
.It Sy -srv_untrusted Em filenames Ns | Ns Em uris
Intermediate CA certs that may be useful when validating client
certificates.
.It Sy -ref_cert Em filename Ns | Ns Em uri
Certificate to be expected for RR messages and any oldCertID in KUR
messages.
.It Sy -rsp_cert Em filename Ns | Ns Em uri
Certificate to be returned as mock enrollment result.
.It Sy -rsp_key Em filename Ns | Ns Em uri
Private key to be returned as central key generation result.
.It Sy -rsp_keypass Em arg
Pass phrase source for
.Sy rsp_cert
and
.Sy rsp_key .
.It Sy -rsp_crl Em filename Ns | Ns Em uri
CRL to be returned in genp of type
.Qo Li crls Qc .
.It Sy -rsp_extracerts Em filenames Ns | Ns Em uris
Extra certificates to be included in mock certification responses.
.It Sy -rsp_capubs Em filenames Ns | Ns Em uris
CA certificates to be included in mock Initialization Response (IP)
message.
.It Sy -rsp_newwithnew Em filename Ns | Ns Em uri
Certificate to be returned in newWithNew field of genp of type
rootCaKeyUpdate.
.It Sy -rsp_newwithold Em filename Ns | Ns Em uri
Certificate to be returned in newWithOld field of genp of type
rootCaKeyUpdate.
.It Sy -rsp_oldwithnew Em filename Ns | Ns Em uri
Certificate to be returned in oldWithNew field of genp of type
rootCaKeyUpdate.
.It Sy -poll_count Em number
Number of times the client must poll before receiving a certificate.
.It Sy -check_after Em number
The checkAfter value (number of seconds to wait) to include in poll
response.
.It Sy -grant_implicitconf
Grant implicit confirmation of newly enrolled certificate.
.It Sy -pkistatus Em number
PKIStatus to be included in server response.
Valid range is 0 (accepted) .. 6 (keyUpdateWarning).
.It Sy -failure Em number
A single failure info bit number to be included in server response.
Valid range is 0 (badAlg) .. 26 (duplicateCertReq).
.It Sy -failurebits Em number No Number representing failure bits to be included in server response. Valid range is 0 .. 2^27 - 1.
.It Sy -statusstring Em arg
Text to be included as status string in server response.
.It Sy -send_error
Force server to reply with error message.
.It Sy -send_unprotected
Send response messages without CMP-level protection.
.It Sy -send_unprot_err
In case of negative responses, server shall send unprotected error
messages, certificate responses (IP/CP/KUP), and revocation responses
(RP). WARNING: This setting leads to behavior violating RFC 4210.
.It Sy -accept_unprotected
Accept missing or invalid protection of requests.
.It Sy -accept_unprot_err
Accept unprotected error messages from client.
So far this has no effect because the server does not accept any error
messages.
.It Sy -accept_raverified
Accept RAVERIFED as proof of possession (POPO).
.El
.Ss Certificate verification options, for both CMP and TLS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -allow_proxy_certs Ns , Sy -attime Ns , Sy -no_check_time Ns , Sy -check_ss_sig Ns , Sy -crl_check Ns , Sy -crl_check_all Ns , Sy -explicit_policy Ns , Sy -extended_crl Ns , Sy -ignore_critical Ns , Sy -inhibit_any Ns , Sy -inhibit_map Ns , Sy -no_alt_chains Ns , Sy -partial_chain Ns , Sy -policy Ns , Sy -policy_check Ns , Sy -policy_print Ns , Sy -purpose Ns , Sy -suiteB_128 Ns , Sy -suiteB_128_only Ns , Sy -suiteB_192 Ns , Sy -trusted_first Ns , Sy -use_deltas Ns , Sy -auth_level Ns , Sy -verify_depth Ns , Sy -verify_email Ns , Sy -verify_hostname Ns , Sy -verify_ip Ns , Sy -verify_name Ns , Sy -x509_strict Sy -issuer_checks
Set various options of certificate chain verification.
See
.Xr openssl-verification-options 1
for details.
.Pp
The certificate verification options
.Sy -verify_hostname ,
.Sy -verify_ip ,
and
.Sy -verify_email
only affect the certificate verification enabled via the
.Sy -out_trusted
option.
.El
.Sh NOTES
When a client obtains, from a CMP server, CA certificates that it is
going to trust, for instance via the
.Qo Li caPubs Qc
field of a certificate response or using general messages with infoType
.Qo Li caCerts Qc
or
.Qo Li rootCaCert Qc ,
authentication of the CMP server is particularly critical.
So special care must be taken setting up server authentication using
.Sy -trusted
and related options for certificate-based authentication or
.Sy -secret
for MAC-based protection.
If authentication is certificate-based, the
.Sy -srvcertout
option should be used to obtain the validated server certificate and
perform an authorization check based on it.
.Pp
When setting up CMP configurations and experimenting with enrollment
options typically various errors occur until the configuration is
correct and complete.
When the CMP server reports an error the client will by default check
the protection of the CMP response message.
Yet some CMP services tend not to protect negative responses.
In this case the client will reject them, and thus their contents are
not shown although they usually contain hints that would be helpful for
diagnostics.
For assisting in such cases the CMP client offers a workaround via the
.Sy -unprotected_errors
option, which allows accepting such negative messages.
.Pp
If OpenSSL was built with trace support enabled (e.g.,
.Qo Li ./config enable-trace Qc )
and the environment variable
.Sy OPENSSL_TRACE
includes
.Sy HTTP ,
the requests and the response headers transferred via HTTP are printed.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Ss Simple examples using the default OpenSSL configuration file
This CMP client implementation comes with demonstrative CMP sections in
the example configuration file
.Pa openssl/apps/openssl.cnf ,
which can be used to interact conveniently with the Insta Demo CA.
.Pp
In order to enroll an initial certificate from that CA it is sufficient
to issue the following shell commands.
.Bd -literal
  export OPENSSL_CONF=/path/to/openssl/apps/openssl.cnf
.Ed
.Bd -literal
  openssl genrsa -out insta.priv.pem
  openssl cmp -section insta
.Ed
.Pp
This should produce the file
.Pa insta.cert.pem
containing a new certificate for the private key held in
.Pa insta.priv.pem .
It can be viewed using, e.g.,
.Bd -literal
  openssl x509 -noout -text -in insta.cert.pem
.Ed
.Pp
In case the network setup requires using an HTTP proxy it may be given
as usual via the environment variable
.Sy http_proxy
or via the
.Sy -proxy
option in the configuration file or the CMP command-line argument
.Sy -proxy ,
for example
.Bd -literal
  -proxy http://192.168.1.1:8080
.Ed
.Pp
In the Insta Demo CA scenario both clients and the server may use the
pre-shared secret
.Em insta
and the reference value
.Em 3078
to authenticate to each other.
.Pp
Alternatively, CMP messages may be protected in signature-based manner,
where the trust anchor in this case is
.Pa insta.ca.crt
and the client may use any certificate already obtained from that CA, as
specified in the
.Sy [signature]
section of the example configuration.
This can be used in combination with the
.Sy [insta]
section simply by
.Bd -literal
  openssl cmp -section insta,signature
.Ed
.Pp
By default the CMP IR message type is used, yet CR works equally here.
This may be specified directly at the command line:
.Bd -literal
  openssl cmp -section insta -cmd cr
.Ed
.Pp
or by referencing in addition the
.Sy [cr]
section of the example configuration:
.Bd -literal
  openssl cmp -section insta,cr
.Ed
.Pp
In order to update the enrolled certificate one may call
.Bd -literal
  openssl cmp -section insta,kur,signature
.Ed
.Pp
using signature-based protection with the certificate that is to be
updated.
For certificate updates, MAC-based protection should generally not be
used.
.Pp
In a similar way any previously enrolled certificate may be revoked by
.Bd -literal
  openssl cmp -section insta,rr -trusted insta.ca.crt
.Ed
.Pp
or
.Bd -literal
  openssl cmp -section insta,rr,signature
.Ed
.Pp
Many more options can be given in the configuration file and/or on the
command line.
For instance, the
.Sy -reqexts
CLI option may refer to a section in the configuration file defining
X.509 extensions to use in certificate requests, such as
.Qo Li v3_req Qc
in
.Pa openssl/apps/openssl.cnf :
.Bd -literal
  openssl cmp -section insta,cr -reqexts v3_req
.Ed
.Ss Certificate enrollment
The following examples do not make use of a configuration file at first.
They assume that a CMP server can be contacted on the local TCP port 80
and accepts requests under the alias
.Em /pkix/ .
.Pp
For enrolling its very first certificate the client generates a client
key and sends an initial request message to the local CMP server using a
pre-shared secret key for mutual authentication.
In this example the client does not have the CA certificate yet, so we
specify the name of the CA with the
.Sy -recipient
option and save any CA certificates that we may receive in the
.Qo Li capubs.pem Qc
file.
.Pp
In below command line usage examples the
.Qo Li \e Qc
at line ends is used just for formatting; each of the command
invocations should be on a single line.
.Bd -literal
  openssl genrsa -out cl_key.pem
  openssl cmp -cmd ir -server 127.0.0.1:80/pkix/ -recipient "/CN=CMPserver" \e
    -ref 1234 -secret pass:1234-5678 \e
    -newkey cl_key.pem -subject "/CN=MyName" \e
    -cacertsout capubs.pem -certout cl_cert.pem
.Ed
.Ss Certificate update
Then, when the client certificate and its related key pair needs to be
updated, the client can send a key update request taking the certs in
.Qo Li capubs.pem Qc
as trusted for authenticating the server and using the previous cert and
key for its own authentication.
Then it can start using the new cert and key.
.Bd -literal
  openssl genrsa -out cl_key_new.pem
  openssl cmp -cmd kur -server 127.0.0.1:80/pkix/ \e
    -trusted capubs.pem \e
    -cert cl_cert.pem -key cl_key.pem \e
    -newkey cl_key_new.pem -certout cl_cert.pem
  cp cl_key_new.pem cl_key.pem
.Ed
.Pp
This command sequence can be repeated as often as needed.
.Ss Requesting information from CMP server
Requesting "all relevant information" with an empty General Message.
This prints information about all received ITAV
.Sy infoType Ns s
to stdout.
.Bd -literal
  openssl cmp -cmd genm -server 127.0.0.1/pkix/ -recipient "/CN=CMPserver" \e
    -ref 1234 -secret pass:1234-5678
.Ed
.Ss Using a custom configuration file
For CMP client invocations, in particular for certificate enrollment,
usually many parameters need to be set, which is tedious and error-prone
to do on the command line.
Therefore, the client offers the possibility to read options from
sections of the OpenSSL config file, usually called
.Pa openssl.cnf .
The values found there can still be extended and even overridden by any
subsequently loaded sections and on the command line.
.Pp
After including in the configuration file the following sections:
.Bd -literal
  [cmp]
  server = 127.0.0.1
  path = pkix/
  trusted = capubs.pem
  cert = cl_cert.pem
  key = cl_key.pem
  newkey = cl_key.pem
  certout = cl_cert.pem

  [init]
  recipient = "/CN=CMPserver"
  trusted =
  cert =
  key =
  ref = 1234
  secret = pass:1234-5678-1234-567
  subject = "/CN=MyName"
  cacertsout = capubs.pem
.Ed
.Pp
the above enrollment transactions reduce to
.Bd -literal
  openssl cmp -section cmp,init
  openssl cmp -cmd kur -newkey cl_key_new.pem
.Ed
.Pp
and the above transaction using a general message reduces to
.Bd -literal
  openssl cmp -section cmp,init -cmd genm
.Ed
